WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.588
Picture a thief with a stolen card number.

2
00:00:05.588 --> 00:00:11.225
A program tests it with small payments at online shops, one every 50 milliseconds.

3
00:00:11.467 --> 00:00:13.884
A very busy, very small shopper.

4
00:00:14.267 --> 00:00:16.507
The payments app has a guard for this.

5
00:00:16.507 --> 00:00:23.112
Before it says yes, it counts the card's payments in the last minute, and refuses when there are more than five.

6
00:00:23.367 --> 00:00:26.642
On one computer, that count takes a millisecond.

7
00:00:26.642 --> 00:00:29.441
But the payments now live on 8 computers.

8
00:00:29.441 --> 00:00:33.808
The team added computers to go faster, and the check got slower.

9
00:00:33.808 --> 00:00:34.081
Why?

10
00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:36.335
Start with one computer.

11
00:00:36.335 --> 00:00:40.647
The payments sit in a table, rows of records, one row per payment.

12
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:46.432
An index is a lookup from a value to where its rows are, like the index at the back of a book.

13
00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:54.408
With an index on the card, the check finds card 242's ten payments in one step, instead of reading every row.

14
00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:57.113
Now split the table over 8 computers.

15
00:00:57.113 --> 00:00:59.983
Each piece, on its own computer, is a shard.

16
00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:05.687
A payment's shard comes from its payment number: hash it, divide by 8, keep the remainder.

17
00:01:05.687 --> 00:01:09.240
The value that picks the shard is called the partition key.

18
00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:13.432
Find a payment by its number, and you ask exactly one shard.

19
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:16.894
But the fraud check doesn't have a payment number.

20
00:01:16.894 --> 00:01:17.788
It has a card.

21
00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:23.017
An index on a column that isn't the partition key is called a secondary index.

22
00:01:23.017 --> 00:01:26.699
And with shards, we have to decide where its entries live.

23
00:01:26.933 --> 00:01:29.580
Picture a city library with eight branches.

24
00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:36.227
Each book sits at one branch, picked by its number, and each branch keeps a catalogue of its own books only.

25
00:01:36.467 --> 00:01:40.303
Adding a book is easy: the branch that gets it files the card.

26
00:01:40.303 --> 00:01:46.677
But to find every book by one author, you phone all eight branches and wait for each librarian to look.

27
00:01:46.967 --> 00:01:50.078
The simplest index for shards works the same way.

28
00:01:50.078 --> 00:01:55.983
The branch is a shard, a book is a payment, and the branch's catalogue is that shard's index.

29
00:01:56.200 --> 00:02:02.122
Shard 4's index knows card 242's payments on shard 4, and nothing else.

30
00:02:02.122 --> 00:02:03.957
This is a local index.

31
00:02:04.233 --> 00:02:05.504
Writes stay simple.

32
00:02:05.504 --> 00:02:10.788
A new payment and its index entry go to the same shard, together or not at all.

33
00:02:10.788 --> 00:02:13.597
So the index is never behind its payments.

34
00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:14.905
Now the read.

35
00:02:14.905 --> 00:02:16.981
Card 242 has ten payments.

36
00:02:16.981 --> 00:02:20.893
How many shards does the fraud check have to ask?

37
00:02:20.893 --> 00:02:22.171
Pause and guess.

38
00:02:25.367 --> 00:02:26.053
All eight.

39
00:02:26.053 --> 00:02:28.457
The ten payments sit on six shards.

40
00:02:28.457 --> 00:02:32.235
But shards 2 and 6 hold none, and they still get asked.

41
00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:33.718
Could we skip them?

42
00:02:33.718 --> 00:02:39.233
Only if someone knew they were empty, and shard 4's index knows nothing about shard 2.

43
00:02:39.467 --> 00:02:42.714
So the check asks every shard and merges the answers.

44
00:02:42.714 --> 00:02:44.920
That's called a scatter-gather read.

45
00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:47.543
And a card with no payments at all?

46
00:02:47.543 --> 00:02:48.346
Still eight.

47
00:02:48.346 --> 00:02:51.961
Seven librarians say not here, and so does the eighth.

48
00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:53.455
Now the cost.

49
00:02:53.455 --> 00:02:59.624
At 2,000 checks a second, that's 16,000 shard requests a second on 8 shards.

50
00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:05.561
Add a ninth shard, and it gets all 2,000 checks a second too, just like the others.

51
00:03:05.561 --> 00:03:09.108
So adding shards adds no capacity for this question.

52
00:03:09.367 --> 00:03:11.799
That's the slower check from the start.

53
00:03:11.799 --> 00:03:16.228
Hiring more librarians doesn't help when every call rings every branch.

54
00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:19.103
A local index has another blind spot.

55
00:03:19.103 --> 00:03:20.986
It can't keep a value unique.

56
00:03:20.986 --> 00:03:26.896
No shard sees the others' entries, so none can tell that a value is already used elsewhere.

57
00:03:27.167 --> 00:03:37.840
MongoDB, a popular database that splits its data over shards, says so in its manual: a unique index works only if it starts with the partition key.

58
00:03:38.133 --> 00:03:42.939
And none of this hurts when the question names the partition key.

59
00:03:42.939 --> 00:03:52.845
Split the payments by shop instead, and card-242's payments at shop M-0042 go to that one shop's shard, and use the local index there.

60
00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:59.272
So: a local index is cheap when a question names the partition key, and costly when it doesn't.

61
00:03:59.867 --> 00:04:04.365
So the index works on 8 machines, but each machine only knows its own.

62
00:04:04.365 --> 00:04:08.413
Ask a question without the partition key, and you ask everyone.

63
00:04:08.667 --> 00:04:11.705
Asking eight shards costs eight times the work.

64
00:04:11.705 --> 00:04:16.424
It also costs time, in a way that surprises most people, and that's next.

65
00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:18.095
One quick check.

66
00:04:18.095 --> 00:04:22.129
The app grows to 32 shards, with the same local index.

67
00:04:22.129 --> 00:04:26.462
How many shard requests do 2,000 checks a second cost now?

68
00:04:26.462 --> 00:04:27.657
Pause and think.

69
00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:31.741
64,000.

70
00:04:31.741 --> 00:04:34.625
Every check asks all 32.
